// Somewhere to put work that is too slow for the loop.
//
// A loop is one thread, and everything on it takes turns. A callback that hashes a password,
// compresses an asset or reads a file the slow way is a callback nothing else runs during -- so
// libuv keeps a pool of threads beside the loop, and `queue` is how a job gets onto one.
//
//     var out: &sync Digest = Digest([0; 32])
//
//     queue(() -> hash_into(out), (r) -> r match
//         Ok(_) -> print(out.bytes[0])
//         Err(e) -> print(s"the job did not run: $e"))?
//
// **Two callbacks, on two threads.** The job runs on a pool thread; the completion runs back on the
// loop's, in its turn, like every other callback in this package. That split is what the whole
// design is about, and it is the reason the two are typed differently.
//
// ## What a job may capture
//
// **A `&sync Fn` is a closure two threads may hold, so everything it captures has to be countable
// atomically** -- and the compiler is what says so, at the closure rather than at run time:
//
//     var c: &Cell = Cell(1)
//
//     queue(() -> print(c.n), on_done)?
//
//     error: a closure shared between two domains may be called from either, so every count it
//     captures has to be atomic -- but the 'c' it captures reaches a '&Cell', whose count is not.
//     Hold it as a '&sync Cell'
//
// So what a job may reach is: **scalars and arrays**, which are copied into it; **`&sync T`**, which
// is the way to share something both threads touch; and **`*T`**, which carries no count and asks
// the writer to have thought about it. A `string`, a slice and an ordinary `&T` are refused, and the
// refusal names which capture it was.
//
// **A `&sync T` makes the reference safe to share, not the object safe to mutate.** Two jobs on the
// pool writing one `&sync` struct race exactly as they would in C; what the type buys is that the
// box itself is not torn apart underneath them. `sysl.sync.Atomic[T]` and `sysl.posix.threads.Mutex`
// are what order the writes.
//
// **The completion callback is an ordinary closure**, because it runs on the loop's thread and
// crosses nothing. That is where a job's answer is read and where it is handed to whatever asked
// for it.
//
// ## How big the pool is
//
// Four threads, unless `UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE` says otherwise -- and libuv reads that **once**, the
// first time anything uses the pool, which includes `resolve` and every file operation given a
// callback. There is no call here that sets it, and the reason is the rule about an API that has to
// be first: a library function cannot promise what the program did before it, so a `set_pool_size`
// would be a promise this package is not in a position to make. A program that wants a different
// pool sets the variable in its environment before it starts.
//
// **The pool is shared**, so a job that blocks for a second is a second in which one of the four
// threads resolves no names and reads no files.

module sh.sysl.libuv

import sh.sysl.libuv.c

// The job a request carries once it has run: something has to be in the field, and a closure that
// does nothing is cheaper to call than an `Option` is to test. It is the `&sync` counterpart of
// `noop` beside it, and it exists for the same reason.
private sync_noop() -> unit = ()

private ignore_outcome(r: Result[unit, Error]) -> unit = ()

/** A job that has been handed to the thread pool.
 *
 * **It is a request rather than a handle**, which is libuv's own distinction and matters here: a
 * request is one operation with an end, so there is no `close` and nothing to keep alive
 * afterwards. Like a handle it holds a reference to itself while it is in flight, so a program need
 * not bind the result of `queue` to a name for the job to run.
 */
struct Work
    private storage: [c.WORK_WORDS]u64
    private lp: &Loop
    private me: Option[&Work]
    private job: &sync Fn() -> unit
    private on_done: &Fn(Result[unit, Error]) -> unit

    private req(*self) -> *c.Work = ptr_cast(&self.storage[0])

    /** The loop the completion callback will run on. */
    on_loop(*self) -> &Loop = self.lp

    /** Whether the job is still the loop's business -- queued, running, or waiting to report.
     *
     * It answers `false` from the moment the completion callback is entered, which is what makes it
     * the guard `cancel` needs.
     */
    is_pending(self) -> bool = self.me.is_some()

    /** Ask for the job not to run.
     *
     * **Only a job that has not started can be cancelled**, and libuv says so with `EBUSY` for one
     * already on a thread. A cancelled job's completion callback still runs, on the loop, with
     * `ECANCELED` -- so a program has one place to release whatever the job was holding whichever
     * way it went.
     *
     * `EINVAL` for a request that has already reported. That check is this side's rather than
     * libuv's: `uv_cancel` on a finished request reads storage libuv has done with, and faults
     * rather than answering.
     */
    cancel(*self) -> Result[unit, Error]
        if !self.is_pending() then return Err(Error(EINVAL))

        checked(c.cancel(ptr_cast(self.req())))
end Work

// Called on a pool thread, and the only place in this package where sysl runs anywhere but the
// loop's own thread. It reads one field and calls it; everything else the request holds belongs to
// the loop, and touching it here would be the race the `&sync` on that field exists to rule out.
private work_run(req: *c.Work) -> unit
    val w: *Work = ptr_cast(c.req_get_data(ptr_cast(req)))

    w.job()

// Back on the loop's thread. Drops both callbacks and the request's reference to itself before
// calling out, exactly as a handle's close does -- the self-reference may be the last one, so the
// callback is read into a local first.
private work_done(req: *c.Work, status: i32) -> unit
    val w: *Work = ptr_cast(c.req_get_data(ptr_cast(req)))
    val cb = w.on_done

    w.job = sync_noop
    w.on_done = ignore_outcome
    w.me = None

    cb(checked(status))

/** Run `job` on one of the loop's worker threads, and call `cb` on the loop's thread when it is
 * over.
 *
 * `Ok` from this call means the job was queued. Whether it ran is what the callback carries:
 * `Ok(())` for a job that ran to the end, and `Err` with `ECANCELED` for one `cancel` caught before
 * a thread picked it up.
 *
 * **A job answers by writing, not by returning.** Its closure is `() -> unit`, because a value
 * coming back out of a pool thread would be a value crossing a domain boundary -- which is the same
 * reason `sysl.posix.threads.Thread.join` does not carry one. What a job has to say it says through
 * a `&sync` it captured, and the completion callback is where that is read.
 *
 * **The job keeps the loop alive while it is queued**, so a program that queues one and calls `run`
 * gets its completion callback rather than an immediate return.
 */
@crossing(job)
queue(job: &sync Fn() -> unit, cb: &Fn(Result[unit, Error]) -> unit = ignore_outcome,
      lp: &Loop = default_loop()) -> Result[&Work, Error]
    var w: &Work = Work([0; c.WORK_WORDS], lp, None, job, cb)

    w.me = Some(w)
    c.req_set_data(ptr_cast(w.req()), ptr_cast(&*w))

    val r = c.queue_work(lp.raw(), w.req(), &work_run, &work_done)

    if r < 0
        w.me = None
        return Err(Error(r))

    Ok(w)
